<h2>The facts alleged</h2>

<p>FIR No. 367/2026 was registered on 19 June 2026 at Police Station Sultanpuri under Section 69 BNS. The complainant, a 24-year-old graduate, connected with the applicant on a social media application in April 2026, when he allegedly told her he was unmarried. On learning he was married she blocked him, then unblocked him after he allegedly persuaded her otherwise. On 22 May 2026 the two were together at her home, where, according to the complaint, physical relations took place on a promise of marriage and the applicant recorded photographs and videos. The same evening her brother saw the applicant at the house; the applicant was later assaulted by her family, and his phone was broken. A week before the present FIR, the applicant\'s wife had lodged her own FIR of 29 May 2026 — a sequence the defence relied upon to describe the present case as a counter-blast. The applicant was arrested on 30 June 2026, and the chargesheet was filed during his custody.</p>

<h2>The court\'s assessment</h2>

<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><h3>The defence</h3><p>A consensual relationship between adults — immoral, given the applicant\'s marriage, but not an offence; the FIR followed his wife\'s complaint and the family confrontation; custody since June with the investigation complete.</p></div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new"><h3>The State</h3><p>Opposition rested on the gravity of the offence; the rival factual narratives on either side were not disputed.</p></div>
</div>

<p>Justice Girish Kathpalia granted bail. Three observations stand out. First, the complainant was a grown-up, educated adult, not a gullible or uneducated person — relevant because Section 69 turns on deceit operating on the complainant\'s decision, and the plausibility of deceit is assessed against her capacity to evaluate the circumstances, including her own discovery, mid-relationship, that the applicant was married. Second, for the purposes of bail only, the relationship appeared consensual. Third, the investigation\'s own gaps were noted: the phone said to contain the photographs and videos had not been sent for forensic retrieval. The court was careful to insulate the trial: the trial court must form its own independent view on the record.</p>

<h2>Section 69 BNS: the emerging bail pattern</h2>

<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Factor</th><th>Bearing on bail in Section 69 cases</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Complainant\'s age, education and circumstances</td><td>Courts ask whether the alleged false promise could realistically have overborne an adult\'s judgment, especially where disqualifying facts (such as an existing marriage) were known</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Timeline of the relationship</td><td>Blocking, unblocking, continued association after discovery — the sequence informs whether consent was induced by deceit or was independent of it</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Cross-complaints</td><td>A preceding FIR by or against relatives of either side invites scrutiny of motive, without deciding it at the bail stage</td></tr>
  <tr><td>State of the investigation</td><td>Chargesheet filed and forensic steps either taken or conspicuously not taken — continued custody serves little investigative purpose once the record is frozen</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The larger point</h2>

<p>Section 69 was enacted to give a specific home to false-promise prosecutions that earlier strained the definition of rape, and it carries a sentence of up to ten years. Precisely because the provision criminalises a species of deception within intimate relationships, bail courts are proving attentive to the line between deceit and a relationship that soured — a line the Supreme Court has long policed in false-promise rape cases by asking whether the promise was false from inception. This order fits the pattern: it neither trivialises the complaint nor allows the gravity of the label to decide custody, and it quietly signals to investigators that electronic allegations require forensic follow-through if they are to carry weight at any stage.</p>

<div class="note"><p>This article summarises a Delhi High Court bail order for general information. Observations made for bail purposes do not decide the truth of the allegations, which remains for trial. Nothing here is legal advice.</p></div>
